@!internal

module nupp.compiler.preludecache

--[[
The checked prelude, as plain data a later command can read back.

Every `nupp` command builds an environment, and building one means parsing and
type-checking the three prelude declaration files from source. That is about a
hundred and thirty milliseconds, and a check of a small project is a hundred and
ninety: nearly all of what a small command costs is the prelude, computed again
from bytes that did not change.

So it is computed once and kept. What `nupp.compiler.env` restores from an image
is eight roots, and this turns those roots into a value `nupp.compiler.build.store`
can hold and back again.

This is not `nupp.compiler.preludeimage`, and the two are not interchangeable.
That one reads a resource generated ahead of time for a host that cannot run the
checker at all, in a text format written by a tool outside the compiler. This one
is a cache: written by the checker that would otherwise be run, read only by a
process that agrees about the compiler and the sources it came from, and thrown
away rather than migrated whenever it might not.

## What has to be flattened

The prelude is a graph, not a tree: types refer to each other, and a receiver
type is a table used as a key. `string.buffer`, which is what the store
serializes with, encodes neither shared references nor cycles. So the graph is
numbered -- every table gets an id, every reference becomes that id -- and the
numbering is undone on the way back. Flat arrays rather than nested records,
because that is the shape `string.buffer` is quickest at and the shape a decoder
can walk without allocating anything it throws away.

`NUPP_NO_PRELUDE_CACHE` in the environment turns all of this off, which is how a
suspected difference between a restored prelude and a checked one is bisected: run
the same thing twice and see which answer changes.

## Identity

A type is not a value that can simply be copied. The checker compares types with
`==`, and `types.literal("#")` answers out of an arena `nupp.compiler.types`
keeps, so a restored type that is in no arena is equal to nothing: with the
prelude copied and nothing else done, the compiler reports eighteen errors
checking itself, every one of them a literal or an overload failing to match
something it is equal to.

Every interned type is therefore written down with the arena and key it was
interned under, and put back there. A key the reading process already has wins --
that object is the one every other reference in it is already using -- and the
image's members are written onto it, because checking the prelude is also what
filled it in.

The serials go with them. `intern` stamps each type with one, and the counter
that issues them starts at zero in every process, so restoring types without
restoring the counter would have this process hand out a serial some restored
type already carries. `types.resumeIdentity` moves the counters forward to where
the writing process left them, never back.

## Identity the compiler already owns

Not everything reachable from the prelude was made by it. `types.any` is one
object the whole checker compares against with `==`, and a record's metatable is
another; there are eighty-odd of them, and copying one produces a value that is
equal to nothing and narrows to nothing. The first attempt at this cache did copy
them, and the compiler stopped being able to check itself.

So a table the compiler already owned is written as the name it is owned under --
its module and the path to it -- and looked up again rather than rebuilt. The
walk that finds them reads every `nupp.` module that is loaded, which is complete
by construction: a module that put one of its objects in the prelude graph is a
module that was loaded to do it.

## Trivia

A token carries `trivia`, which is the arena its file's comments and whitespace
live in, and the arena is not plain data. It is also not derivable from the
tokens, and dropping it would take the prelude's doc comments with it -- which is
what a hover over a standard-library name shows.

An arena is a source string and a run of five-integer records, so it is written
out as exactly that and built again by replaying the records into a new one. Two
of them cover the whole prelude.
]]

local triviaarena = require("nupp.compiler.triviaarena")
local types = require("nupp.compiler.types")

local preludecache = {}

--- The roots `nupp.compiler.env` restores, in the order they are written.
---
--- `preludeRuntime` is not here: it is a string, so it needs no numbering and is
--- carried beside the graph.
local ROOT_NAMES = {
    "annotationsByName",
    "featureEffects",
    "globalTypeDefs",
    "globalTypes",
    "globals",
    "preludeComptimeFunctions",
    "stringLib",
}

--- Bumped when the shape below changes. An image that does not say this number is
--- discarded, the same way a store entry stamped differently is.
local FORMAT = 1

local function isScalar(value: any): boolean
    local kind = type(value)

    return kind == "boolean" or kind == "number" or kind == "string"
end

--- How deep into a module's exports the ownership walk goes.
---
--- Deep enough for `types.builtins.string`, which is two, with room to spare. A
--- bound rather than none, because a module is free to hold a graph of its own and
--- this is a walk of tables reached by name, not of everything that exists.
local WALK_DEPTH = 8

--- That the `require` in `resolveOwned` is reflection, not a dependency, for
--- `nupp.compiler.build.cache`'s module graph.
---
--- The name it resolves came out of an image this process is deciding whether to
--- believe, and a name that no longer resolves to the same object discards the image.
--- So the modules reached there are not code this one is defined by, and the stamp
--- covering this need not cover them -- which would be the whole compiler, since the
--- walk above takes every loaded `nupp.` module.
@allow("unused-binding")
const DYNAMIC_REQUIRES = "@requires-dynamically nothing"

--- Every table a loaded compiler module owns, and the name it owns it under.
---
--- Keys are sorted so the walk names an object the same way in the process that
--- writes an image and the one that reads it. Both ends run this; a name either
--- resolves to the same object in both or the image is discarded.
local function ownedByModules(): {[any]: {string}}
    local owned: {[any]: {string}} = {}
    local moduleNames: {string} = {}
    for name in pairs(package.loaded) do
        if type(name) == "string" and name:match("^nupp%.") then
            moduleNames[#moduleNames + 1] = name
        end
    end
    table.sort(moduleNames)

    local function claim(value: any, moduleName: string, path: string, depth: integer): nil
        if type(value) ~= "table" or owned[value] ~= nil or depth > WALK_DEPTH then
            return
        end
        owned[value] = {moduleName, path}
        local keys: {string} = {}
        for key in pairs(value as any) do
            if type(key) == "string" then
                keys[#keys + 1] = key
            end
        end
        table.sort(keys)
        for _, key in ipairs(keys) do
            claim((value as any)[key], moduleName, path == "" and key or (path .. "." .. key), depth + 1)
        end
    end

    for _, moduleName in ipairs(moduleNames) do
        claim(package.loaded[moduleName], moduleName, "", 0)
    end

    return owned
end

--- The object a written name refers to, or nil when it no longer resolves.
local function resolveOwned(entry: any): any
    if type(entry) ~= "table" or type(entry[1]) ~= "string" or type(entry[2]) ~= "string" then
        return nil
    end
    local loaded = package.loaded[entry[1] as string]
    if loaded == nil then
        local ok, required = pcall(require, entry[1] as string)
        if not ok then
            return nil
        end
        loaded = required
    end
    local value: any = loaded
    if entry[2] ~= "" then
        for part in (entry[2] as string):gmatch("[^%.]+") do
            if type(value) ~= "table" then
                return nil
            end
            value = value[part]
        end
    end
    if type(value) ~= "table" then
        return nil
    end

    return value
end

-- What a cell holds.
local SCALAR: integer = 0
local REFERENCE: integer = 1
local OWNED: integer = 2

--- Turns the roots into flat plain data, or nil when something in them cannot be.
---
--- Nil rather than an error, because a prelude this cannot hold is a prelude that
--- is computed the slow way -- which is what every command did before this
--- existed. The alternative, a half-written image, is the one outcome that would
--- be worse than no cache.
function preludecache.encode(roots: any): any?
    local owned = ownedByModules()
    local identity = types.identity()

    -- Where each interned type lives. A table found here is one the checker will
    -- look for by key, so it is written out with that key rather than as an
    -- anonymous copy -- and unlike a module's own furniture, it is walked into,
    -- because the prelude is what filled it in.
    local internedAt: {[any]: {string}} = {}
    for bucket, entries in pairs(identity.arenas) do
        for key, value in pairs(entries) do
            if internedAt[value] == nil then
                internedAt[value] = {bucket, key}
            end
        end
    end
    local externs: {any} = {}
    local externIds: {[any]: integer} = {}
    local ids: {[any]: integer} = {}
    local tables: {any} = {}
    local pending: {any} = {}
    local arenaIds: {[any]: integer} = {}
    local arenas: {any} = {}
    local metas: {[integer]: integer} = {}
    local metaExterns: {[integer]: integer} = {}
    local triviaOf: {[integer]: integer} = {}

    local function externFor(value: any): integer
        local known = externIds[value]
        if known ~= nil then
            return known
        end
        local id = #externs + 1
        externs[id] = owned[value]
        externIds[value] = id

        return id
    end

    local function identify(value: any): nil
        if type(value) ~= "table" or ids[value] ~= nil then
            return
        end
        -- A module's own furniture is referred to, never rebuilt. An interned type
        -- is rebuilt even when a module also happens to name it, because its
        -- members are the prelude's work.
        if owned[value] ~= nil and internedAt[value] == nil then
            return
        end
        local id = #tables + 1
        ids[value] = id
        tables[id] = value
        pending[#pending + 1] = value
    end

    local function arenaFor(arena: any): integer?
        local known = arenaIds[arena]
        if known ~= nil then
            return known
        end
        local source = (arena as any).source
        local count = (arena as any).count
        if type(source) ~= "string" or type(count) ~= "number" then
            return nil
        end
        local records: {number} = {}
        for index = 1, count as integer do
            local kind, offset, length, line, col = (arena as any):record(index)
            local at = #records
            records[at + 1] = kind
            records[at + 2] = offset
            records[at + 3] = length
            records[at + 4] = line
            records[at + 5] = col
        end
        local id = #arenas + 1
        arenas[id] = {source = source, records = records}
        arenaIds[arena] = id

        return id
    end

    for _, name in ipairs(ROOT_NAMES) do
        -- A root the compiler already owns would mean the prelude never made one,
        -- which is not a prelude this knows how to restore.
        if owned[roots[name]] ~= nil then
            return nil
        end
        identify(roots[name])
    end

    -- And from the arenas, not only from the roots.
    --
    -- Checking the prelude interns types that nothing in its eight roots reaches:
    -- eleven types, twenty packs and a capability, on the prelude as it stands.
    -- Reachability is the wrong question to ask about them. An interned type is
    -- one the checker will later look for by key, and a key that finds nothing is
    -- a second type built under it -- a different object for a type that is
    -- supposed to be the same one, which is exactly what interning exists to
    -- prevent. Left out, they were the whole of a divergence that took sixty test
    -- cases to show up.
    for _, entries in pairs(identity.arenas) do
        for _, value in pairs(entries) do
            identify(value)
        end
    end

    local at: integer = 1
    while at <= #pending do
        local owner = pending[at]
        local id = ids[owner] as integer
        local trivia = (owner as any).trivia
        if trivia ~= nil then
            local arena = arenaFor(trivia)
            if arena == nil then
                return nil
            end
            triviaOf[id] = arena
        end
        for key, value in pairs(owner as any) do
            if key ~= "trivia" then
                if not isScalar(key) and type(key) ~= "table" then
                    return nil
                end
                if not isScalar(value) and type(value) ~= "table" then
                    return nil
                end
                identify(key)
                identify(value)
            end
        end
        local meta = getmetatable(owner)
        if meta ~= nil then
            if type(meta) ~= "table" then
                return nil
            end
            if owned[meta] ~= nil and internedAt[meta] == nil then
                metaExterns[id] = externFor(meta)
            else
                identify(meta)
                metas[id] = ids[meta] as integer
            end
        end
        at = at + 1
    end

    -- One pass to number, one to write, because a member reached late still has to
    -- be written as the id it was given.
    local counts: {integer} = {}
    local cells: {any} = {}
    local tags: {integer} = {}
    local written: integer = 0

    local function writeCell(value: any): nil
        written = written + 1
        if owned[value] ~= nil and internedAt[value] == nil then
            tags[written] = OWNED
            cells[written] = externFor(value)
        elseif ids[value] ~= nil then
            tags[written] = REFERENCE
            cells[written] = ids[value]
        else
            tags[written] = SCALAR
            cells[written] = value
        end
    end

    for id = 1, #tables do
        local members: integer = 0
        for key, value in pairs(tables[id] as any) do
            if key ~= "trivia" then
                members = members + 1
                writeCell(key)
                writeCell(value)
            end
        end
        counts[id] = members
    end

    local rootIds: {integer} = {}
    for index, name in ipairs(ROOT_NAMES) do
        rootIds[index] = ids[roots[name]] or 0
    end

    local internedAs: {[integer]: {string}} = {}
    for value, where in pairs(internedAt) do
        local id = ids[value]
        if id ~= nil then
            internedAs[id as integer] = where
        end
    end

    return {
        format = FORMAT,
        counts = counts,
        cells = cells,
        tags = tags,
        externs = externs,
        metas = metas,
        metaExterns = metaExterns,
        arenas = arenas,
        triviaOf = triviaOf,
        internedAs = internedAs,
        identity = {serial = identity.serial, capability = identity.capability, nominal = identity.nominal,},
        roots = rootIds,
        runtime = roots.preludeRuntime,
    }
end

--- Builds the roots again from what `encode` wrote, or nil when the image is not
--- one this understands.
---
--- Guarded throughout: the image comes off disk, where anything could have
--- written it, and every way it can be wrong has the same answer as no image at
--- all.
function preludecache.decode(image: any): any?
    if type(image) ~= "table" or image.format ~= FORMAT then
        return nil
    end
    local counts = image.counts
    local cells = image.cells
    local tags = image.tags
    if type(counts) ~= "table" or type(cells) ~= "table" or type(tags) ~= "table" then
        return nil
    end

    local externs: {any} = {}
    for index, entry in ipairs(image.externs or {}) do
        local resolved = resolveOwned(entry)
        -- A name that no longer resolves is a compiler that moved under the image.
        if resolved == nil then
            return nil
        end
        externs[index] = resolved
    end

    -- Nothing is written until everything has been checked, because some of what
    -- gets written to is live: a type the reading process already interned is
    -- filled in from the image rather than replaced, and an image that turned out
    -- to be unreadable half way through would leave that type half rewritten.
    local expected: integer = 0
    for id = 1, #counts do
        local members = counts[id]
        if type(members) ~= "number" or members < 0 then
            return nil
        end
        expected = expected + (members as integer) * 2
    end
    if expected ~= #cells or #tags ~= #cells then
        return nil
    end
    for index = 1, #cells do
        local tag = tags[index]
        if tag == REFERENCE then
            local target = cells[index]
            if type(target) ~= "number" or target < 1 or target > #counts then
                return nil
            end
        elseif tag == OWNED then
            local target = cells[index]
            if type(target) ~= "number" or externs[target as integer] == nil then
                return nil
            end
        elseif tag ~= SCALAR then
            return nil
        end
    end

    -- Which object each id is. A key this process already interned answers with
    -- the type it is already using; everything else is new.
    --
    -- And a type that was already there is left exactly as it is. That is what
    -- `intern` does -- it returns the type in the arena and discards the one its
    -- caller just built -- and a second environment in one process has to mean the
    -- same thing whether it checked the prelude or read it.
    --
    -- Writing the image's members onto it instead is a rewiring, not a refresh:
    -- the second environment's members refer to the second environment's copies of
    -- everything that is not interned, so the first environment's types come away
    -- pointing into the second environment's graph. The two then disagree about
    -- what an owned value is, which showed up as a dropped value the checker could
    -- not see being dropped.
    local built: {any} = {}
    local mine: {[integer]: boolean} = {}
    local adopting: {integer} = {}
    for id = 1, #counts do
        local where = (image.internedAs or {})[id]
        local live = nil
        if type(where) == "table" and type(where[1]) == "string" and type(where[2]) == "string" then
            live = types.interned(where[1] as string, where[2] as string)
            if live == nil then
                adopting[#adopting + 1] = id
            end
        end
        built[id] = live or {}
        mine[id] = live == nil
    end

    local at: integer = 1
    local function readCell(): any
        local tag = tags[at]
        local cell = cells[at]
        at = at + 1
        if tag == REFERENCE then
            return built[cell as integer]
        elseif tag == OWNED then
            return externs[cell as integer]
        end

        return cell
    end

    for id = 1, #counts do
        local target = mine[id] and built[id] or nil
        for _ = 1, counts[id] as integer do
            local key: any = readCell()
            local value: any = readCell()
            if key == nil then
                return nil
            end
            if target ~= nil then
                target[key] = value
            end
        end
    end
    for id, meta in pairs(image.metas or {}) do
        local owner = mine[id as integer] and built[id as integer] or nil
        local metatable = built[meta as integer]
        if metatable == nil then
            return nil
        end
        if owner ~= nil then
            setmetatable(owner, metatable)
        end
    end
    for id, extern in pairs(image.metaExterns or {}) do
        local owner = mine[id as integer] and built[id as integer] or nil
        local metatable = externs[extern as integer]
        if metatable == nil then
            return nil
        end
        if owner ~= nil then
            setmetatable(owner, metatable)
        end
    end

    local rebuilt: {any} = {}
    for index, arena in ipairs(image.arenas or {}) do
        if type(arena.source) ~= "string" or type(arena.records) ~= "table" then
            return nil
        end
        local made = triviaarena.new(arena.source)
        local records = arena.records
        local count = #records
        local cursor: integer = 1
        while cursor + 4 <= count do
            made:append(
                records[cursor] as integer,
                records[cursor + 1] as integer,
                records[cursor + 2] as integer,
                records[cursor + 3] as integer,
                records[cursor + 4] as integer
            )
            cursor = cursor + 5
        end
        rebuilt[index] = made
    end
    for id, arena in pairs(image.triviaOf or {}) do
        local owner = mine[id as integer] and built[id as integer] or nil
        local made = rebuilt[arena as integer]
        if made == nil then
            return nil
        end
        if owner ~= nil then
            owner.trivia = made
        end
    end

    -- Interned last, so a type only enters an arena once it is complete.
    for _, id in ipairs(adopting) do
        local where = (image.internedAs or {})[id]
        types.adopt(where[1] as string, where[2] as string, built[id])
    end
    -- And the counters with them, or the next type this process interns is stamped
    -- with a serial one of these already carries.
    types.resumeIdentity(image.identity)

    local roots: any = {}
    for index, name in ipairs(ROOT_NAMES) do
        local id = (image.roots or {})[index]
        if id == nil then
            return nil
        end
        roots[name] = id ~= 0 and built[id as integer] or nil
    end
    roots.preludeRuntime = image.runtime

    return roots
end

export = preludecache
